7592186577324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57edc055-3dc9-5acb-8736-c0c617f4d5fc" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=- 4 e^{\\frac{y}{2}},\\qquad 3 x + 2>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f\\prime(x)=- \\frac{4}{3 x + 2}.$ is false. Differentiating $f(x)=- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=-2\\cdot\\frac{3}{3 x + 2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{6}{3 x + 2} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv - \\frac{4}{3 x + 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is false. Substituting $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=- \\frac{6}{3 x + 2}$ into the original equation gives expressions that are not identical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}- 4 e^{\\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ - \\frac{6}{3 x + 2} &\\overset{?}{=}-4e^{-(- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)})/(-2)} \\\\ - \\frac{6}{3 x + 2} &\\overset{?}{=}-4e^{-\\ln(3 x + 2)} \\\\ - \\frac{6}{3 x + 2} &\\not\\equiv - \\frac{4}{3 x + 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is false. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is false, the function $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 2 \\right)}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486 :topic/id #uuid "f8bc5d87-e505-5270-9efd-ad77aaf46e52" :topic/math-academy-id 1181 :topic/title "Verifying Solutions of Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ac82169-4017-5aee-b8a2-b79e679b0722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577295} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1fbee2-4889-5e84-9f46-037cf43d3d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ac82169-4017-5aee-b8a2-b79e679b0722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577295} :answer-field/id #uuid "4712b020-9702-5c3c-a3ff-cabe5f1d6dfc" :answer-field/key "Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577291} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d576c80-7571-50d0-bc26-368ad4cb8fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577286} {:answer/id #uuid "42fb3203-d6f2-5685-9a07-82e33c2e8163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42fb3203-d6f2-5685-9a07-82e33c2e8163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577289} :answer-field/id #uuid "61085ad0-ae6c-5fc4-974f-fb93aeef646c" :answer-field/key "$f\\prime(x)=\\frac{12}{4 x + 4}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577297} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ad3c8a-d899-5f30-9f69-f2953849fa2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577309} {:answer/id #uuid "9abd5e60-f344-53f9-9b87-6cfd5386e6ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ad3c8a-d899-5f30-9f69-f2953849fa2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577309} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3ad4a5-9e8c-538b-8578-c03c5ca4643c" :answer-field/key "The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a098865b-a0f4-5d9b-a304-bd75a306b510" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=6 e^{- \\frac{y}{3}},\\qquad 4 x + 4>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{12}{4 x + 4}.$ is true. Differentiating $f(x)=3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=3\\cdot\\frac{4}{4 x + 4} \\\\ &=\\frac{12}{4 x + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is false. Substituting $y=3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{12}{4 x + 4}$ into the original equation gives expressions that are not identical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}6 e^{- \\frac{y}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{4 x + 4} &\\overset{?}{=}6e^{-(3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)})/(3)} \\\\ \\frac{12}{4 x + 4} &\\overset{?}{=}6e^{-\\ln(4 x + 4)} \\\\ \\frac{12}{4 x + 4} &\\not\\equiv \\frac{3}{2 \\left(x + 1\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is false. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is false, the function $y=3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{12}{4 x + 4}.$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486 :topic/id #uuid "f8bc5d87-e505-5270-9efd-ad77aaf46e52" :topic/math-academy-id 1181 :topic/title "Verifying Solutions of Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06be9c0a-0223-57ac-aa2a-f18ff8de1f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577280} {:answer/id #uuid "6d228363-cf1a-5ff6-9768-677aa5961cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d228363-cf1a-5ff6-9768-677aa5961cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759