6b137d2-ab8b-583a-a33b-00f008bb4c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577328} :answer-field/id #uuid "54de8a04-1471-5f5a-8ae7-f7320ab59390" :answer-field/key "$f\\prime(x)=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ea016ba9-b08a-5130-a64c-616daed418ef" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=6 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}},\\qquad 3 x + 1>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}.$ is true. Differentiating $f(x)=2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=2\\cdot\\frac{3}{3 x + 1} \\\\ &=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true. Substituting $y=2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}6 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{3 x + 1} &\\overset{?}{=}6e^{-(2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)})/(2)} \\\\ \\frac{6}{3 x + 1} &\\overset{?}{=}6e^{-\\ln(3 x + 1)} \\\\ \\frac{6}{3 x + 1} &=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is true. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true, the function $y=2 \\ln{\\left(3 x + 1 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{6}{3 x + 1}.$; “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement”; “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation”."}]
